#!/bin/sh
# Post-install for the `.deb` and the `.rpm` alike.
#
# The package delivered `/usr/libexec/classis/supervisor` and nothing else. The install itself —
# the versioned layout, the `current` pointer, the systemd unit — belongs to the Client's own
# `service install`, which is all this calls. No package ships a unit file: one that did
# would know nothing of the Client's service name or its restart policy, and there would be two
# registrations disagreeing about which is the real one.
#
# What an operator invokes on PATH is a symlink through the layout's `current` pointer,
# laid here — so `classis --version` answers for the binary the service runs, not for whatever
# this package happened to deliver. The link carries the **product's** name and the file it resolves
# to carries the program's: two variant builds must not collide on one
# `/usr/bin` entry, and they would if the link were named after the program they share.
#
# The paths name the default system roots (`default_layout_root` and `default_root` in
# crates/classis-agent/src/service/manager.rs), the only roots a packaged install ever uses: this script
# never passes `--root`. They are two roots on Linux — the executable layout under `/opt`, because
# SELinux never lets systemd execute a binary labeled `var_lib_t`; the configuration and state under
# `/var/lib`, where data belongs.
#
# The service runs under the system account `classis`. It is created here
# where it is missing — on every run, also on a host that stays root, so that moving such a host is
# one command — and never modified or removed. Only a first registration, where no unit exists, is
# told the account; every later one runs `service install` without `--run-as`, which keeps the
# account the unit names. A host that chose root with `--run-as root` stays root.
#
# There is no migration branch here: an upgrade is a re-registration, so there is nothing to carry
# across.
#
# dpkg calls this as `postinst configure <old-version>`, rpm as `%post <count>`. The two argument
# conventions have nothing in common, so nothing here reads "$1" except to bow out of dpkg's abort
# paths — everything else is idempotent by design and safe on either.
set -e

case "${1-}" in
abort-upgrade | abort-remove | abort-deconfigure) exit 0 ;;
esac

# Asked *before* `service install` runs, because install swings the `current` pointer and stops
# nothing: a host that was already running is one under management being upgraded, and its process
# still holds the previous binary while the pointer now names the new one.
running=no
if command -v systemctl >/dev/null 2>&1 &&
	systemctl is-active --quiet classis.service; then
	running=yes
fi

if ! getent passwd classis >/dev/null 2>&1; then
	# A group of that name without its account is joined, since `--user-group` would refuse it.
	if getent group classis >/dev/null 2>&1; then
		useradd --system --gid classis --no-create-home --home-dir /var/lib/classis \
			--shell /usr/sbin/nologin classis
	else
		useradd --system --user-group --no-create-home --home-dir /var/lib/classis \
			--shell /usr/sbin/nologin classis
	fi
fi

if [ -e /etc/systemd/system/classis.service ]; then
	/usr/libexec/classis/supervisor service install
else
	/usr/libexec/classis/supervisor service install --run-as classis
fi

# `-n`: without it, ln would place the link *inside* the directory an existing link resolves to.
# On rpm upgrades the erasure of the old package's files deletes this link again; `%posttrans`
# (packaging/linux/posttrans) re-lays it after that erasure, which dpkg does not need — it removes
# the obsolete `/usr/bin` file during unpack, before this runs.
ln -sfn /opt/classis/current/supervisor /usr/bin/classis

if [ "$running" = yes ]; then
	# Restart, never start. A service that was running was configured by somebody, and it has to
	# finish this upgrade on the binary the package just delivered rather than on the one it still
	# has mapped.
	systemctl restart classis.service
	exit 0
fi

cat <<'EOF'

The service is registered, stopped, and does not start at boot. Not starting it is deliberate: it
has no configuration yet, and this host is not enrolled, which the Client needs before it runs
anything. Two steps remain:

  classis service install --interactive
  classis enrol https://<server> <key>

The first asks for the endpoint and the Server's CA, and writes supervisor.toml.
`classis service config` asks again later.

The second registers this host with the Server using the key of its Space, and then starts the
service and lets it start at boot. Without a terminal, put the fleet's files in a directory and
name it:

  classis service install --endpoint wss://<server>/v1/opamp --fleet-files /root/fleet

To write the file yourself instead, give it at least the endpoint:

  endpoint = "wss://<server>/v1/opamp"

The configuration lets the Server update this Client itself, from signed packages only
— the fleet then patches its own agent like any other program. Write `self_update = false` under
[packages] in supervisor.toml to keep that one update manual.

A new installation runs the service, and every Agent it supervises, as the system account
classis, and every upgrade keeps the account the unit names. An Agent that needs root (GLPI
Agent's full inventory, a Telegraf input without a sudo rule, a port below 1024) needs a sudo rule,
a drop-in under /etc/systemd/system/classis.service.d/, or root, chosen with:

  classis service install --run-as root
EOF
